    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Executive Verdict & Compensation Breakdown\n\nThis is an **exceptionally strong, top-of-band offer** for an L6 Product Manager at Google. \n\nGoogle typically utilizes a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for initial GSUs (historically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** depending on the grant structure). \n\nHere is how the numbers compare assuming Google's standard 33/33/22/12 schedule vs. your current Meta compensation:\n\n| Year | Meta (Current) | Google Offer | Google Breakdown |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | ~$476,000 | **~$954,200** | $260k Base + $52k Bonus + $442.2k RSU (33%) + $200k Sign-on |\n| **Year 2** | ~$526,000 | **~$754,200** | $260k Base + $52k Bonus + $442.2k RSU (33%) + *Refresher 1* |\n| **Year 3** | ~$526,000 | **~$606,800** | $260k Base + $52k Bonus + $294.8k RSU (22%) + *Refreshers 1 & 2* |\n| **Year 4** | ~$526,000 | **~$472,800** | $260k Base + $52k Bonus + $160.8k RSU (12%) + *Refreshers 1, 2 & 3* |\n| **4-Yr Average** | ~$513,500/yr | **~$697,000/yr** | *(Excluding any stock appreciation or refreshers)* |\n\n*(Even under an even 25%/year vesting model, your Year 1 would be **$847,000**, with an average annual guaranteed package of **$697,000**).*\n\n---\n\n### 2. How the Components Benchmark (L6 PM SF Bay Area)\n\n* **Base Salary ($260,000):** The posted band is $183k\u2013$271k. At $260k, you are already sitting at the **88th percentile** of the internal range.\n* **New-Hire RSUs ($1,340,000 / 4 yrs):** Median L6 PM stock grants are typically between $600,000 and $900,000 over 4 years ($150k\u2013$225k/yr). At **$335,000/yr annualized**, this equity package is in the **top 5% (95th+ percentile)** for Google L6 PMs.\n* **Sign-On Bonus ($200,000):** Standard L6 sign-on bonuses range from $50,000 to $100,000. A $200k upfront bonus is designed to offset your upcoming $200k Meta vest and eliminate any hesitation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Nuance: The Front-Loaded Schedule & Refresher Stacking\n\n* **The Year 3\u20134 Taper:** Because Google front-loads new-hire equity (33% in Year 1 and 2, dropping to 22% in Year 3 and 12% in Year 4), your initial grant cash flow significantly drops in Years 3 and 4.\n* **How Refreshes Fix This:** Google grants equity refreshes annually (typically in Q1 during the performance cycle). Google refreshes generally vest evenly over 4 years (25%/yr) or front-load depending on the policy year. By Year 3 and 4, stacking 2\u20133 annual refresher grants is designed to fill the gap and keep your ongoing total compensation above $600k\u2013$650k/yr.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommendation: Negotiate (Lightly)\n\n**Do not walk away**, as this offer represents a **~35% to 80%+ increase in total compensation** over Meta across your first 4 years. \n\nBecause you were passively recruited and are happy at Meta, you have strong leverage. While Google is already near the ceiling of L6 bands, there is minimal downside to making **one clear, high-leverage closing counter-offer**.\n\n#### What to Ask For:\n1. **Base Salary:** Ask for the top of the band: **$271,000** (+$11,000).\n2. **Equity:** Ask to round the 4-year grant to an even **$1,500,000** (+$160,000 total / ~$40k/yr).\n\n#### Negotiation Script / Framing:\n> *\"Thank you again for putting this offer together. I\u2019m really excited about the team, the scope, and what we could build together. \n> \n> Because I wasn't actively looking and have strong momentum and upcoming vests at Meta, I want to make sure I\u2019m completely set up for the long term. If we can get the base salary to the top of the posted range at **$271,000** and adjust the equity to **$1.5M over 4 years**, I am ready to sign the offer immediately.\"*\n\n### Final Takeaway\nIf Google holds firm and refuses to adjust the numbers, **you should still accept**. The package is already top-tier for an L6 PM across the industry.",
